@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix mesh: <http://invincible-agent/mesh#> .
@prefix docs: <http://invincible-agent/docs#> .

# =============================================================================
# engine-docs — the OUTPUT end of Contract D. ADR-0037, slice 1.
# =============================================================================
#
# ONE CLASS, AND THE INPUT END IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. Contract D requires `input_uri` AND
# `output_uri` to resolve to real `:OntologyClass` nodes before the registrar accepts a verb, and
# the measured failure it prevents is authoring only one end: the planning extension wrote the
# input end while the output end lived elsewhere, and twelve registrations earned twelve 422s
# naming five URIs — while the engine served /health normally throughout.
#
# For `mesh:explain` the input end is `mesh:DocPage`, which is PLATFORM vocabulary and already
# declared in `mesh_system.ttl` (verified in the deployed graph 2026-09-12: one node, labelled
# OntologyClass). It is not restated here. That is the one case where both-ends-in-one-file does
# not apply — the subject is a platform concept the doc model introduced, not a noun this engine
# owns, and copying it into a domain file would be a domain file claiming a platform term.
#
# THE RESPONSE SHAPE IS IN THE ENGINE'S OWN NAMESPACE AND ITS OWN FILE, which is the rule Engine P
# broke by authoring its outputs as `mesh:*`. Practically it is also an edit to a shared file that
# other lanes are queued on.
#
# `rdfs:comment` ONLY — never `skos:definition`. The Jena-to-Weaviate sync UNIONs the two and the
# last row wins, so a `skos:definition` silently clobbers the comment, and `rdfs:comment` is what
# lands in `Weaviate.OntologyClass.definition`.

docs:DocExplanation a owl:Class ;
  rdfs:subClassOf mesh:Response ;
  rdfs:label "Doc Explanation" ;
  rdfs:comment "The answer returned when someone asks HOW TO DO something or WHAT SOMETHING MEANS and the system has a reviewed write-up covering it: the prose itself, carried whole, with the persona it was written for and the checks it names. IT IS THE WRITING, NOT A RETELLING OF IT — a generated precis would be a second artifact that nothing reviewed and that drifts from the original silently, so what a reader receives is what an author wrote and a reviewer approved. CARRIED WITH THE FINGERPRINT OF THE TEXT AS INDEXED, and refused outright when the two disagree: showing prose that is not what was catalogued is wrong in the one way a reader cannot detect, since the wrong write-up is fluent, well-formed and roughly on topic. AN EMPTY ONE IS A REAL ANSWER — when nothing covers what was asked, that is a gap in the writing rather than a failure of the question, and saying so plainly beats improvising, because nobody writes up a task nobody has performed yet." .
